# A135 — the Metal kernels, where MLX looks for them (2026-09-15)

## The statement was false, and the artefact says so
# `tools/make-app.sh` and `tools/fetch-metal.sh` both said mlx-swift's SwiftPM build does not compile
# the Metal kernels. Under Xcode 27 with the Metal toolchain installed it does:
$ ls .build/out/Intermediates.noindex/mlx-swift.build/Debug/mlx-swift_Cmlx-b.build/Metal/ | wc -l
27
$ swift AUDIT/baseline/swift64/a135-probe/metallib-probe.swift \
      .build/out/Products/Release/mlx-swift_Cmlx.bundle/Contents/Resources/default.metallib
functions: 382
steel: 18
first: rbits, rbitsc, vjp_layer_normfloat16

# The build's metallib and the downloaded one are different files, and the build's is larger:
#   build   3 836 652 bytes  0214ad23cc2fa1df…
#   release 3 789 548 bytes  4cb8ef6cbb43e43f…   (the pinned Cmlx.xcframework asset)

## What the download was actually for: MLX has to be able to *find* a metallib
# `mlx/backend/metal/device.cpp:136-180`, in order:
#   1. `mlx.metallib` beside the running binary
#   2. `Resources/mlx.metallib` beside the running binary
#   3. a SwiftPM resource bundle, via `Bundle.main.bundleURL` then `Bundle.allBundles()`
#   4. `Resources/default.metallib` beside the running binary
#   5. the compile-time METAL_PATH
# The app's engine is `ChatBots.app/Contents/MacOS/chatbots-cli`, a bare executable that
# `make-app.sh` gives the SwiftPM bundles at `Contents/Resources/` — a place (3) does not reach from
# there. So the engine needs a colocated metallib, which is what the fetch put in
# `Contents/MacOS/`.

## The measurement that decides which metallib can be installed
# A first version of `AuditS2MetalLibraryTests` installed the *build's* metallib beside the running
# test binary and evaluated a kernel:
$ swift test --filter MetalLibraryTests          # with the build's metallib colocated
  ✔ Test "An MLX kernel runs ..." passed after 0.889 seconds.
$ swift test --filter MetalLibraryTests          # with nothing colocated
  MLX error: Failed to load the default metallib. library not found library not found library not
  found library not found  at .../mlx-c/mlx/c/array.cpp:232
  ✘ Test run failed.

# So the build's own metallib runs a Metal kernel, and a colocated file is what MLX needs. That is
# what makes replacing the download with a copy possible at all.
#
# That first version was replaced, and the reason is worth keeping: installing a file into the test
# bundle's `Contents/MacOS/` breaks the *build*, because the next `codesign` of that bundle fails —
# "code object is not signed at all. In subcomponent: …/ChatBotsCoreTests.xctest/Contents/MacOS/
# mlx.metallib". It is the same trap `make-app.sh` records at its signing step, met from the other
# side. The tests that ship read the artefact instead of moving it.

## `make-app.sh` now installs the build's metallib
$ bash tools/make-app.sh
  ==> Metal kernels from the build: default.metallib
  ==> Ad-hoc signing
  Built: dist/ChatBots.app                                            (2m07s, no download)
$ find dist/ChatBots.app -name '*.metallib'
  Contents/MacOS/mlx.metallib
  Contents/Resources/mlx-swift_Cmlx.bundle/Contents/Resources/default.metallib
$ shasum -a 256 dist/ChatBots.app/Contents/MacOS/mlx.metallib \
              .build/out/Products/Release/mlx-swift_Cmlx.bundle/Contents/Resources/default.metallib
  0214ad23cc2fa1df… (both: the app carries the metallib its own build produced)
# The pinned, digest-checked download in `tools/fetch-metal.sh` stays as the branch taken when the
# build produced no metallib, which is why that script is still here and still fails closed (A24,
# A185). A normal build no longer downloads ~190 MB.

## What was not verified here, and why
# Full inference against the build's metallib needs a checkpoint, and this host has none (`models/` is
# absent, and there is no Hugging Face cache). What is verified is that the library opens, contains
# MLX's kernels — including the steel attention kernels the download was said to be needed for — and
# runs a real GPU kernel. If a phase-E host has weights, `chatbots-cli --check` against this app
# bundle closes the remaining gap.

